From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:15:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 16/17] open the audio sink on a device and a format the card has

The arriving audio is 197 packets a second and the measured run dropped 190
of them out of the play queue every second, which says the sink at the end
of that queue took almost none of it. Two things are at fault and both say
so:

  snd_pcm_open("default")              = -112 ("Host is down")
  snd_pcm_set_params("hw:0,0", float)  = -22

"default" is the UCM alias, and the alias wants a sound card with a saved
asound state; the card here is an STM32MP25-DK I2S HiFi that answers to
"sysdefault" and to "hw:0,0". The first carries FLOAT_LE and S32_LE, the
second refuses float and S32_LE with -22 and carries S16_LE. So the sink
now takes the configured name first and then the names the card does
answer, and it asks every one of them for the first sample format it
accepts. The samples are f32 and a card usually is not, so what is not
already in the card's format is converted on the way out; float to
integer casts saturate, which is what a stream louder than 1.0 needs
rather than a wrapped sample.

The mode is BLOCK rather than NONBLOCK. A NONBLOCK stream that was never
started answers -EIO, and the measured NONBLOCK stream wrote 0.1 s of
audio and then ended in -EIO, which is the queue drop the diagnostics
show. BLOCK drains 100.5 per cent of real time on both of the names this
board does answer, which is what the arriving audio needs to lose nothing.

Greppable markers: opennow-alsa-sink
